::Q1::When are hallucinations more likely?
{ 
=When questions involve obscure, highly specific, or recent information
~Only when a prompt contains no text
~When the user asks a simple formatting question
~Only when the model is offline
}

::Q2::Why can asking for a citation still produce a hallucination? 
{
~Citations are always verified automatically
=A model may generate a plausible-looking citation even when a real source does not support it
~A citation request disables generation
~Models can only cite real sources
}

::Q3::What responsibility remains with the user? 
{
~Maintaining the model's servers
~Training the model from scratch
=Verifying important generated information before relying on it
~Ensuring the model never generates text
}

::Q4::Which prompt practice can reduce fabrication risk? 
{
=Grounding the model's response in material or facts that are actually provided
~Requesting invented citations
~Asking for increasingly confident wording
~Removing all context
}

::Q5::What should a developer do with an AI-generated technical claim that matters to production? 
{
~Publish it immediately
~Skip testing because the wording is confident
=Check the claim before using it
~Treat it as automatically verified
}